===Etymology 2===
From {{proto|Germanic|trewōn|lang=ang}}, from {{PIE.|ang}} *''drew-o-'', a suffixed variant of ''*drew-, *deru-'' ‘solid, steadfast’, whence probably also PIE noun for "tree" {{proto|Indo-European|dóru|lang=ang|title=}}.  Germanic cognates include Old Frisian ''trē'', Old Saxon ''treo'', Old Norse ''[[tré]]'' (Danish ''[[træ]]''), Gothic {{term|sc=Goth|𐍄𐍂𐌹𐌿|tr=triu|lang=got}}. The IE root is also the source of Ancient Greek '''{{polytonic|[[δόρυ]]}}''' ‘wood, spear’, '''{{polytonic|[[δρῦς]]}}''' ‘tree, oak’, Old Church Slavonic ''[[дрѣво]]'' (Russian ''[[дерево]]''), Old Irish ''[[daur]]'' (Welsh ''[[derwen]]'' ‘oak’).

